From ffed75656d611a1352e75453ffe90019998b39c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 13:42:19 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 15/18] daemon: ration time spent retrying, not wall clock

Three things wrong with the serving allowance, one fix.

It was 10s against a default `jsonrpc_timeout` of 15s, and it was measured from the
start of the call, so it was already gone by the time the opening attempt could fail.
Every failure gave up without retrying. That is why a wallet with history below the
prune height failed outright rather than waiting. Underneath it, electrs stopped
waiting at 15s while the proxy keeps working on the block until its own
`peer_timeout`, 30s by default, and caches what it fetches -- so the fetch being
abandoned was often one the proxy went on to complete.

Raised to 60s, which spans several attempts and keeps trying past that 30s, so a
later attempt finds the block already cached. A proxy that also collapses concurrent
requests for the same block turns that into a join rather than a second fetch: an
improvement, not a requirement.

Raising it alone would not have bounded what it claims to. A run is several RPCs --
prune height, every height, then each block -- each retrying on its own fresh
allowance, so a source that recovers just long enough to get past each stage could
burn it over again at the next. Serving now shares one allowance across the run.

But sharing it must not be done with a deadline, which is what I tried first and had
to undo. A deadline from the start of the run charges time spent *succeeding*: a
query that had been fetching happily for longer than the allowance hit its first
transient error already past the deadline and gave up without a single retry. On a
deep history over Tor, running longer than the allowance is ordinary, so that turned
a stale peer connection into exactly the "failed to fetch history" this change exists
to remove. A slow query is not a failing query.

So what is rationed is time spent retrying, measured from the first failure and
charged to the run as each call finishes. A run that is only succeeding has spent
nothing, however long it has been going. Indexing keeps a fresh allowance per call,
where it exists to ride out a restart rather than bound a wait, and where a
2000-block Tor batch outlasts anything worth setting.

Concurrent fetchers each charge their own retrying, so the total charged can exceed
the allowance while several are in flight. Intended: they retry at the same time, so
the wall clock they cost is still about one allowance.

What waiting costs is in the comment rather than waved away. The preceding commit
makes a batch cost its slowest client instead of the sum, but `handle_events` still
runs inline on the loop that drives `rpc.sync()`, so raising this raises the
worst-case indexing stall -- for a failing query. A succeeding one holds the loop for
as long as it takes, and always has.
