+/// Serving a client can afford to wait, but less.
+///
+/// This used to be 10s, and 10s was below `jsonrpc_timeout` (15s by default). The allowance was
+/// measured from the start of the call, so it was already gone by the time the opening attempt
+/// could fail, and every failure gave up without retrying. That is what made a wallet with
+/// history below the prune height fail outright rather than wait. It is now measured from the
+/// first failure, so it means what its name says: how long to keep *retrying*.
+///
+/// **What waiting still costs.** `handle_events` runs inline on the `server.rs` loop that also
+/// drives `rpc.sync()`. Answering the clients within one batch is parallel, so a batch costs its
+/// slowest client rather than the sum of them, but the loop is still held for that long: a slow
+/// query stalls indexing, and a request arriving mid-batch waits for the batch to end. Raising
+/// this from 10s to 60s therefore raises the worst-case stall with it. Taking the work off that
+/// loop entirely is the fix, and it is not done here. Note that this bounds only a *failing*
+/// query: one that is succeeding holds the loop for as long as it takes, and always has.
+///
+/// That trade is deliberate. The stall is bounded, costs nothing permanent -- indexing resumes
+/// from where it was -- and only happens when fetches are actually failing, whereas the 10s
+/// bought a wallet that could not load its history at all.
+///
+/// Sized against what is on the other end. A single attempt is capped by `jsonrpc_timeout`, but
+/// the proxy in front of a pruned node keeps working on the block for its own `peer_timeout` (30s
+/// by default) after we have stopped waiting, and it caches what it fetches. So the budget is set
+/// to span several attempts and reach past that 30s: a later attempt picks up the block the
+/// earlier one gave up on. A proxy that also collapses concurrent requests for the same block
+/// makes that a join rather than a second fetch, which is a further improvement and not a
+/// requirement.
+///
+/// Shared across the whole run rather than granted per call -- see [`Retry`] for why, and for why
+/// only time spent retrying is counted against it.
+const RETRY_BUDGET_QUERY: Duration = Duration::from_secs(60);
+/// How long to wait before the first retry; doubles up to [`RPC_RETRY_MAX_DELAY`].
+const RPC_RETRY_FIRST_DELAY: Duration = Duration::from_secs(1);
 const RPC_RETRY_MAX_DELAY: Duration = Duration::from_secs(30);
 
+/// How the retry allowance is spent across the several RPCs one run of blocks needs.
+///
+/// A run is not one call. It reads the prune height, resolves every block's height, and then
+/// fetches each block, and each of those retries on its own.
+///
+/// What is counted is **time spent retrying**, never wall clock. That distinction is the whole
+/// point and it is easy to get wrong: an earlier version of this gave serving a single deadline
+/// from the start of the run, which quietly meant a query that had been *succeeding* for longer
+/// than the allowance gave up on its first transient error without retrying at all. On a deep
+/// history over Tor, succeeding for longer than the allowance is the normal case, so that turned
+/// a stale peer connection into exactly the "failed to fetch history" this release exists to fix.
+/// A slow query is not a failing query, and only a failing one should be cut short.
+#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
+enum Retry<'a> {
+    /// One allowance for the whole run.
+    ///
+    /// For serving, where the run holds the `server.rs` loop and a failing one must give up once
+    /// rather than once per stage. Without sharing, a source that is entirely down -- on a pruned
+    /// node one process answers all three kinds of call -- could burn the allowance again at each
+    /// stage that recovers just long enough to get past it.
+    ///
+    /// Workers fetching concurrently each charge their own retrying, so the total charged can
+    /// exceed the allowance while several are in flight. That is intended: they are retrying at
+    /// the same time, so the wall clock they cost is still about one allowance.
+    Shared {
+        spent_nanos: &'a AtomicU64,
+        budget: Duration,
+    },
+    /// A fresh allowance for each call.
+    ///
+    /// For indexing, where it exists to ride out a restart of the block source rather than to
+    /// bound a wait. A 2000-block batch over Tor outlasts any sane allowance, and the last blocks
+    /// in a batch must be as able to survive a blip as the first.
+    PerCall { budget: Duration },
+}
+
+impl Retry<'_> {
+    /// Would `extra` more spent retrying the call in hand exhaust the allowance?
+    fn exhausted(&self, extra: Duration) -> bool {
+        match self {
+            Retry::Shared {
+                spent_nanos,
+                budget,
+            } => Duration::from_nanos(spent_nanos.load(Ordering::Relaxed)) + extra >= *budget,
+            Retry::PerCall { budget } => extra >= *budget,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Record retrying that has finished, so the rest of the run sees it.
+    fn charge(&self, spent: Duration) {
+        if let Retry::Shared { spent_nanos, .. } = self {
+            spent_nanos.fetch_add(spent.as_nanos() as u64, Ordering::Relaxed);
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// The retry loop, with nothing of `Daemon` in it so it can be tested on its own.
+///
+/// `interrupt` is polled between backoff slices rather than once per attempt, so a shutdown
+/// arriving mid-wait is honoured within a fraction of a second rather than after the whole delay.
+///
+/// `first_delay` is a parameter only so tests need not sleep for real seconds. Production always
+/// passes [`RPC_RETRY_FIRST_DELAY`].
+fn retry_with<T>(
+    what: &str,
+    retry: Retry<'_>,
+    first_delay: Duration,
+    interrupt: &dyn Fn() -> Result<()>,
+    mut f: impl FnMut() -> Result<T>,
+) -> Result<T> {
+    // Started at the first failure, not at the first attempt: what is rationed is time spent
+    // retrying. A call that simply takes a long time and then works has cost nothing.
+    let mut retrying_since: Option<Instant> = None;
+    let mut delay = first_delay;
+    let mut attempt = 0usize;
+    loop {
+        match f() {
+            Ok(value) => {
+                if let Some(since) = retrying_since {
+                    retry.charge(since.elapsed());
+                    info!("{} succeeded after {} retries", what, attempt);
+                }
+                return Ok(value);
+            }
+            Err(err) => {
+                let since = *retrying_since.get_or_insert_with(Instant::now);
+                let spent = since.elapsed();
+                if retry.exhausted(spent) {
+                    retry.charge(spent);
+                    return Err(err).with_context(|| {
+                        format!("{} still failing after {}s of retries", what, spent.as_secs())
+                    });
+                }
+                attempt += 1;
+                warn!("{} failed ({:#}); retrying in {}s", what, err, delay.as_secs());
+                // Wait in slices rather than one long sleep, so `interrupt` is seen promptly.
+                let until = Instant::now() + delay;
+                loop {
+                    interrupt()?;
+                    let now = Instant::now();
+                    if now >= until {
+                        break;
+                    }
+                    std::thread::sleep(std::cmp::min(Duration::from_millis(250), until - now));
+                }
+                delay = std::cmp::min(delay * 2, RPC_RETRY_MAX_DELAY);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// Which allowance a run of blocks gets, before the run has somewhere to keep its tally.
+#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
+enum RetryPolicy {
+    Run(Duration),
+    PerCall(Duration),
+}
